## Project: COLG Forum Word Cloud Spider

### Tech Stack
- **Runtime**: Node.js (CommonJS)
- **Server**: Express.js
- **Scraping**: Puppeteer, Cheerio, request-promise
- **Database**: MongoDB via Mongoose
- **NLP**: nodejieba (Chinese segmentation)
- **Frontend**: Vanilla JS + WordCloud2
- **Testing**: Jest + Supertest
- **Linting**: ESLint + Prettier

### Architecture
- `server/index.js` — Entry point, starts Express and spider
- `server/app.js` — Express app factory (testable, no side effects)
- `server/const/_variables.js` — Configuration constants
- `server/db/db.js` — Mongoose connection and model definitions
- `server/spider/` — Web scraping pipeline
- `public/` — Frontend static assets
- `test/` — Test suites (unit + integration)

### Code Conventions
1. Use `const` by default; use `let` only when reassignment is needed; never use `var`
2. Use async/await over raw Promises where possible
3. All new functions must have JSDoc comments
4. All exported functions must have corresponding unit tests in `test/`
5. Keep modules focused: one responsibility per file
6. Use environment variables for configuration that changes between environments
7. Mock external dependencies (DB, network) in tests

### Testing Standards
- Unit tests go in `test/unit/`
- Integration tests go in `test/integration/`
- Test files follow `<module>.test.js` naming
- Mock DB and network calls; never hit real services in tests
- Aim for >50% code coverage on new code
- Run `npm test` before committing

### Git Workflow
- Feature branches: `feature/<description>`
- Bug fixes: `fix/<description>`
- Commit messages follow conventional commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`
- Run lint + tests before pushing

### When Modifying This Project
- Always read the existing code before making changes
- Run `npm test` to verify nothing is broken
- If adding a new spider module, follow the pattern in `server/spider/util.js`
- If adding a new route, add it in `server/app.js` and add an integration test
- Keep the frontend simple — no build tools needed for `public/`
